check.check-content:
Step 1 Legacy.list format (per-line check) Run the following command to find uncommented deb / deb-src entries that lack a Signed-By option:
# grep -PRn -- '^\h*(deb(-src)?)\h+(\[(?![^\]]*\bSigned-By\b)[^\]]*\]|[^\[])'
\
/etc/apt/sources.list \
/etc/apt/sources.list.d/*.list 2>/dev/null
Nothing should be returned.
Note: This command checks each deb / deb-src line individually. It reports lines that either have no options bracket at all, or have an options bracket that does not include Signed-By. Commented lines ( #) are excluded.
Step 2 deb822.sources format (per-stanza check) Run the following script to find stanzas that have a URIs: field but no Signed-By: field:
#!/usr/bin/env bash
{
shopt -s nullglob
while IFS= read -r -d $'\0' l_file; do
awk '
BEGIN { uris=0; signed=0; lineno=0 }
/^[[:blank:]]*$/ {
if (uris && !signed)
print FILENAME ": stanza ending near line " lineno " is missing
Signed-By"
uris=0; signed=0
}
/^[[:blank:]]*URIs:[[:blank:]]*\S/ { uris=1 }
/^[[:blank:]]*Signed-By:[[:blank:]]*\S/ { signed=1 }
{ lineno=NR }
END { if (uris && !signed)
print FILENAME ": final stanza missing Signed-By" }
' "$l_file"
done < <(find /etc/apt/sources.list.d/ -maxdepth 1 -type f -name
'*.sources' -print0)
}
Nothing should be returned.

Run the following command and verify that the APT options for weak dependencies are disabled:
# /usr/bin/apt-config dump | /usr/bin/grep "APT::Install-"
Verify the output matches the following
APT::Install-Recommends "0";
APT::Install-Suggests "0";
Note: If no output is returned, this should be considered a failure as the default behavior is to install packages marked as recommended.

- IF - chrony is in use on the system, run the following script to ensure chrony is configured with an authorized timeserver: Internal Only - General #!/usr/bin/env bash { a_output=() a_output2=() a_config_files=("/etc/chrony/chrony.conf") l_include='(confdir|sourcedir)' l_parameter_name='(server|pool)' l_parameter_value='.+' while IFS= read -r l_conf_loc; do l_dir="" l_ext="" if [ -d "$l_conf_loc" ]; then l_dir="$l_conf_loc" l_ext="*" elif grep -Psq '/\*\.([^#/
]+)?\h*$' <<< "$l_conf_loc" || [ -f "$(readlink -f "$l_conf_loc")" ]; then l_dir="$(dirname "$l_conf_loc")" l_ext="$(basename "$l_conf_loc")" fi if [[ -n "$l_dir" && -n "$l_ext" ]]; then while IFS= read -r -d $'\0' l_file_name; do [ -f "$(readlink -f "$l_file_name")" ] && a_config_files+=("$(readlink -f "$l_file_name")") done < <(find -L "$l_dir" -type f -name "$l_ext" -print0 2>/dev/null) fi done < <(awk '$1~/^\s*'"$l_include"'$/{print $2}' "${a_config_files[*]}" 2>/dev/null) for l_file in "${a_config_files[@]}"; do l_parameter_line="$(grep -Psi '^\h*'"$l_parameter_name"'(\h+|\h*:\h*)'"$l_parameter_value"' ' "$l_file")" [ -n "$l_parameter_line" ] && a_output+=(" - Parameter: "$(tr -d '()' <<< ${l_parameter_name//|/ or })"" \ " Exists in the file: "$l_file" as:" "$l_parameter_line") done [ "${#a_output[@]}" -le "0" ] && a_output2+=(" - Parameter: "$(tr -d '()' <<< ${l_parameter_name//|/ or })"" \ " Does not exist in the chrony configuration") if [ "${#a_output2[@]}" -le 0 ]; then printf '%s
' "" "- Audit Result:" " ** PASS **" "${a_output[@]}" "" else printf '%s
' "" "- Audit Result:" " ** FAIL **" " - Reason(s) for audit failure:" "${a_output2[@]}" fi }

Rule.fixtext:
Note: CIS password complexity requirements may differ from other frameworks or policies, adherence to site-specific policy is imperative. Run the following command: # grep -Pl -- ' pam_pwquality\.so\h+([^#
]+\h+)?(minclass|[dulo]credit) ' /usr/share/pam-configs/* Edit any returned files and remove the minclass, dcredit, ucredit, lcredit, and ocredit arguments from the pam_pwquality.so line(s) Create or modify a file ending in.conf in the /etc/security/pwquality.conf.d/ directory or the file /etc/security/pwquality.conf and add or modify the following line(s) to set complexity according to local site policy: • minclass = _N_ • dcredit = _N_ # Value should be either 0 or a number proceeded by a minus ( - ) symbol • ucredit = -1 # Value should be either 0 or a number proceeded by a minus ( -) symbol • ocredit = -1 # Value should be either 0 or a number proceeded by a minus ( -) symbol • lcredit = -1 # Value should be either 0 or a number proceeded by a minus ( -) symbol Example 1 - Set minclass = 3: #!/usr/bin/env bash { sed -ri 's/^\s*minclass\s*=/# &/' /etc/security/pwquality.conf sed -ri 's/^\s*[dulo]credit\s*=/# &/' /etc/security/pwquality.conf [ ! -d /etc/security/pwquality.conf.d/ ] && mkdir /etc/security/pwquality.conf.d/ printf '
%s' "minclass = 3" > /etc/security/pwquality.conf.d/50- pwcomplexity.conf } Example 2 - set dcredit = -1, ucredit = -1, and lcredit = -1: Internal Only - General #!/usr/bin/env bash { sed -ri 's/^\s*minclass\s*=/# &/' /etc/security/pwquality.conf sed -ri 's/^\s*[dulo]credit\s*=/# &/' /etc/security/pwquality.conf [ ! -d /etc/security/pwquality.conf.d/ ] && mkdir /etc/security/pwquality.conf.d/ printf '%s
' "dcredit = -1" "ucredit = -1" "lcredit = -1" > /etc/security/pwquality.conf.d/50-pwcomplexity.conf }

Edit or add the following line in a *.conf file in /etc/security/pwquality.conf.d or in /etc/security/pwquality.conf:
Example:
#!/usr/bin/env bash
{
[ ! -d /etc/security/pwquality.conf.d/ ] && mkdir
/etc/security/pwquality.conf.d/
printf '\n%s\n' "enforce_for_root" > /etc/security/pwquality.conf.d/50-
pwroot.conf
}

check.check-content:
Run the following script to verify that TMOUT is configured to: include a timeout of no more than 900 seconds, to be readonly, to be exported, and is not being changed to a longer timeout. Internal Only - General #!/usr/bin/env bash { a_output=(); a_output2=(); l_tmout_set="900" f_tmout_read_chk() { a_out=(); a_out2=() l_tmout_readonly="$(grep -P -- '^\h*(typeset\h\- xr\hTMOUT=\d+|([^#
]+)? readonly\h+TMOUT )' "$l_file")" l_tmout_export="$(grep -P -- '^\h*(typeset\h\- xr\hTMOUT=\d+|([^#
]+)? export ([^#
]+ )?TMOUT )' "$l_file")" if [ -n "$l_tmout_readonly" ]; then a_out+=(" - Readonly is set as: "$l_tmout_readonly" in: "$l_file"") else a_out2+=(" - Readonly is not set in: "$l_file"") fi if [ -n "$l_tmout_export" ]; then a_out+=(" - Export is set as: "$l_tmout_export" in: "$l_file"") else a_out2+=(" - Export is not set in: "$l_file"") fi } while IFS= read -r l_file; do l_tmout_value="$(grep -Po -- '^([^#
]+)? TMOUT=\d+ ' "$l_file" | awk - F= '{print $2}')" f_tmout_read_chk if [ -n "$l_tmout_value" ]; then if [[ "$l_tmout_value" -le "$l_tmout_set" && "$l_tmout_value" -gt "0" ]]; then a_output+=(" - TMOUT is set to: "$l_tmout_value" in: "$l_file"") [ "${#a_out[@]}" -gt 0 ] && a_output+=("${a_out[@]}") [ "${#a_out2[@]}" -gt 0 ] && a_output2+=("${a_out[@]}") fi if [[ "$l_tmout_value" -gt "$l_tmout_set" || "$l_tmout_value" -le "0" ]]; then a_output2+=(" - TMOUT is incorrectly set to: "$l_tmout_value" in: "$l_file"") [ "${#a_out[@]}" -gt 0 ] && a_output2+=(" ** Incorrect TMOUT value **" "${a_out[@]}") [ "${#a_out2[@]}" -gt 0 ] && a_output2+=("${a_out2[@]}") fi else [ "${#a_out[@]}" -gt 0 ] && a_output2+=(" - TMOUT is not set" "${a_out[@]}") [ "${#a_out2[@]}" -gt 0 ] && a_output2+=(" - TMOUT is not set" "${a_out2[@]}") fi done < <(grep -Pls -- '^([^#
]+)? TMOUT ' /etc/*bashrc /etc/profile /etc/profile.d/*.sh) [[ "${#a_output[@]}" -le 0 && "${#a_output2[@]}" -le 0 ]] && a_output2+=(" - TMOUT is not configured") if [ "${#a_output2[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then printf '%s
' "Non-compliant TMOUT configuration:" "${a_output2[@]}" fi } Nothing should be returned. Any output identifies a file where TMOUT is missing, incorrectly set, not marked readonly, or not exported. Internal Only - General Note: If TMOUT is set as readonly through readonly TMOUT and/or typeset -xr in more than once, you will receive an error message when logging into a terminal session or connecting with openSSH. It is recommended that TMOUT be set only once in only one file.

Note: The -w option is deprecated and the rule should be updated in accordance with the Remediation Procedure. 1. Run the following command to verify a rule exists in the running configuration: # auditctl -l | grep -Ps -- '^\h*[^#
]+\h*/usr/bin/kmod' Verify the output includes: -a always,exit -F path=/usr/bin/kmod -F perm=x -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset 2. Run the following command to verify a rule exists in a rules file: # grep -Ps -- '^\h*[^#
]+\h*/usr/bin/kmod' /etc/audit/rules.d/*.rules Example output: /etc/audit/rules.d/50-kernel_modules.rules:-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -F path=/usr/bin/kmod -F perm=x -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k kernel_modules Symlink audit Run the following script to audit if the symlinks kmod accepts are indeed pointing at it: Internal Only - General #!/usr/bin/env bash { a_files=("/usr/sbin/lsmod" "/usr/sbin/rmmod" "/usr/sbin/insmod" "/usr/sbin/modinfo" "/usr/sbin/modprobe" "/usr/sbin/depmod") for l_file in "${a_files[@]}"; do if [ "$(readlink -f "$l_file")" = "$(readlink -f /bin/kmod)" ]; then printf "OK: "$l_file"
" else printf "Issue with symlink for file: "$l_file"
" fi done } Verify the output states OK. If there is a symlink pointing to a different location it should be investigated

Run the following script and verify no results are returned:
#!/usr/bin/env bash
{
while read -r l_count l_uid; do
if [ "$l_count" -gt 1 ]; then
echo -e "Duplicate UID: \"$l_uid\" Users: \"$(awk -F: '($3 == n) {
print $1 }' n=$l_uid /etc/passwd | xargs)\""
fi
done < <(cut -f3 -d":" /etc/passwd | sort -n | uniq -c)
}

Run the following script and verify no results are returned:
#!/usr/bin/env bash
{
while read -r l_count l_gid; do
if [ "$l_count" -gt 1 ]; then
echo -e "Duplicate GID: \"$l_gid\" Groups: \"$(awk -F: '($3 == n) {
print $1 }' n=$l_gid /etc/group | xargs)\""
fi
done < <(cut -f3 -d":" /etc/group | sort -n | uniq -c)
}

Run the following script and verify no results are returned:
#!/usr/bin/env bash
{
while read -r l_count l_user; do
if [ "$l_count" -gt 1 ]; then
echo -e "Duplicate User: \"$l_user\" Users: \"$(awk -F: '($1 == n) {
print $1 }' n=$l_user /etc/passwd | xargs)\""
fi
done < <(cut -f1 -d":" /etc/passwd | sort -n | uniq -c)
}

Run the following script and verify no results are returned:
#!/usr/bin/env bash
{
while read -r l_count l_group; do
if [ "$l_count" -gt 1 ]; then
echo -e "Duplicate Group: \"$l_group\" Groups: \"$(awk -F: '($1 ==
n) { print $1 }' n=$l_group /etc/group | xargs)\""
fi
done < <(cut -f1 -d":" /etc/group | sort -n | uniq -c)
}
